patches.behavior:
Stateless, apart from a per-instance warned-once flag used by the edge-case warning below.

Each frame, for each loop index:
```
n = ctx.inputCount
if (ctx.typeParam === "text") {
  out = "";
  for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) out += ctx.input(`value${i}`);   // coerced to text at connect time
} else {
  out = ctx.input("value1");
  for (i = 2; i <= n; i++) out = out + ctx.input(`value${i}`);   // component-wise
}
ctx.output("output", out);
```

- Variants: `number` is a single component; `point` and `size` have 2 components, `point3d` 3, `point4d` 4. Vector variants apply the operation to each component independently. Coercion happens at connect time (ARCHITECTURE.md §4): a number wired into a vector variant broadcasts to every component, and a boolean arrives as 1 or 0.
- Text: joins in port order with no separator. Numbers wired into the text variant are formatted by the engine's number → text coercion; empty inputs join as "".
- Input count: `ctx.inputCount` is 2–32 (default 2). Removing ports drops their values; added ports start at the variadic default.
- Frame 0: evaluates with the authored values. There's no history, so nothing is seeded.
- Loops: evaluates once per loop index. The output length is the longest input loop; shorter loops wrap and scalars broadcast. An empty loop produces an empty output.
- Edge cases: in numeric variants, any result component that isn't finite (NaN or ±Infinity, e.g. from overflow past ±1.8e308) outputs 0 for that component and warns once per restart through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)`. `-0` is output as `0`.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears the warned-once flag. Nothing to dispose.
- Coming from Origami: + folds its inputs the same way. Its type menu also lists index, progress, and anchor. Import index and progress as `number` and anchor as `point`.

Stateless, apart from a per-instance warned-once flag used by the edge-case warning below.

`points` is a whole-loop port, so the patch evaluates once per frame (not once per loop index). It reads every other input with `ctx.inputItems` and zips them itself (see Loops).

For each zipped item `i`:
```
v = value[i]; vel = velocity[i];
r = deceleration[i] === "fast" ? 0.99 : 0.998;        // decay per millisecond, same as scroll momentum
projected = v + vel * r / (1000 * (1 - r));           // normal: v + 0.499·vel; fast: v + 0.099·vel
if (mode[i] === "step") {
  for each component c:
    s = Math.abs(step[i][c]);
    if (s === 0) { out[c] = projected[c]; n[c] = 0; }  // axis left free
    else { n[c] = roundHalfAwayFromZero((projected[c] - offset[i][c]) / s); out[c] = offset[i][c] + n[c] * s; }
  index = n[first component whose step isn't 0] ?? 0;
} else {                                              // "points"
  pts = ctx.inputItems("points");
  if (pts.length === 0) { out = projected; index = -1; }
  else { k = argmin over pts of distance(projected, pts[k]); out = pts[k]; index = k; }
}
emit output[i] = out, index[i] = index, projected[i] = projected
```

- Projection: the distance a POP-style decay (velocity multiplied by `r` every millisecond) travels before stopping: `(vel / 1000) · r / (1 − r)`. It equals where a Scroll momentum fling at that velocity would come to rest without bounds, so snapping and scrolling agree. Velocity 0 skips projection exactly.
- Distance: `|a − b|` for `number`; Euclidean `√Σ(aᶜ − bᶜ)²` for `point`. Ties go to the lowest index. Step-mode ties (exactly halfway) round away from Offset.
- Variants: `value`, `velocity`, `step`, `offset`, `points`, `output`, and `projected` follow the variant. A number wired into the `point` variant broadcasts to both components.
- Negative steps: the sign is ignored (`|step|`).
- Frame 0: evaluates with the authored values. There's no history, so velocity comes only from the input.
- Loops: `points` is always taken whole. `value`, `velocity`, `mode`, `step`, `offset`, and `deceleration` zip: the output length is the longest of those loops, shorter loops wrap, and scalars broadcast. Every item snaps against the whole `points` loop. Outputs are loops only when one of the zipped inputs is a loop; otherwise they're scalars. If a zipped input is an empty loop, all outputs are empty loops.
- Retargeting: stateless, so the output jumps as soon as Value changes. Feed it into an animation for motion.
- Edge cases: any output component that isn't finite (from an extreme velocity) outputs 0 and warns once per restart through `ctx.services.log("warn", …)`. `-0` is output as `0`.
- Muted: the default bypass: `output` and `projected` pass `value` through, and `index` outputs 0.
- Restart and dispose: restart clears the warned-once flag. Nothing to dispose.
